<title>PORTUGAL: World copper demand/supply balanced Jan-Sept - ICSG.</title>
<headline>World copper demand/supply balanced Jan-Sept - ICSG.</headline>
<dateline>LISBON 1996-12-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>World copper supply and demand were more or less in balance in the first nine months of 1996 with figures pointing to a surplus of just 36,000 tonnes, the Lisbon-based International Copper Study Group (ICSG) said in its latest report.</p>
<p>Mine and refined copper production rose 6.4 percent and 11 percent, respectively, compared with the same period last year. But consumption was slightly lower.</p>
<p>By the end of October, refined stocks on the LME and COMEX metal exchanges were down to 150,200 tonnes, compared with a high of 295,500 tonnes in August, it said.</p>
<p>World refined copper consumption and supply trends in thousands of tonnes for the first nine months-</p>
<p>				    1996 (Jan-Sept)  1995 (p)(Jan-Sept)</p>
<p> World consumption		     9,132		9,207</p>
<p> World mine capacity		   9,063		8,418</p>
<p> World mine production		 7,960		7,477</p>
<p> Percent capacity utilisation	 87.8		 88.8</p>
<p> Primary refined production	  7,858		7,104</p>
<p> Secondary refined production	1,310		1,664</p>
<p> Refined production		    9,168		8,768</p>
<p> Percent scrap in total		 14.3		 19.0</p>
<p> Refined stocks end of period (1)    589		  600</p>
<p> Four weeks of world consumption     937		  944</p>
<p> Refined surplus/deficit (2)	    36		 (440)</p>
<p>NOTE - p refers to preliminary data. 1. Refined stocks include those held in the exchanges, producers, consumers, merchants and governments in western economies. 2. Surplus or deficit is calculated using total refined production minus refined consumption.</p>
<p>-- Lisbon editorial 3511-3538254</p>
